7. Workstream B — Data-retention policy model

Design the complete retention-policy data model and policy lifecycle.

Inspect existing schema and project documentation before choosing table names or relationships. Reuse established naming conventions and avoid duplicate policy models.

The plan must specify whether a dedicated "governance.retention_policies" table and related execution-history tables are appropriate, and define their exact proposed responsibilities.

B.1 Policy definition

Specify the policy fields and constraints required to represent:

- Policy identifier and stable policy code.
- Human-readable name and purpose.
- Record category or supported target.
- Retention duration or another explicitly justified retention rule.
- Retention reference point, such as creation time or event time.
- Permitted disposition: deletion, anonymization, archival or another justified action.
- Enabled/disabled status.
- Policy version or equivalent change history.
- Creation and modification accountability.
- Effective dates where necessary.
- Legal, operational or other approved retention rationale.
- Any protected categories that require stricter controls.

Define which policy combinations are valid and which must be rejected.

Do not invent statutory retention periods. Where a legal or regulatory requirement has not been established, flag it as a decision requiring confirmation rather than presenting a guessed period as law.

B.2 Policy lifecycle and governance

Specify permitted transitions and business rules for:

- Creating a policy.
- Reviewing and activating a policy.
- Editing an existing policy.
- Disabling or superseding a policy.
- Handling an invalid policy.
- Preserving historical policy versions and decision accountability.

Determine which changes take effect immediately and which require an explicit effective date or controlled activation.

A policy modification must not silently rewrite the historical explanation of why an earlier retention operation occurred.

Define safeguards against accidentally disabling all applicable retention controls or activating a policy that would dispose of protected data.

B.3 Initial policy coverage

Inventory the existing data categories and recommend a complete initial policy catalogue appropriate to the actual repository.

Evaluate, where present:

- Central audit records.
- Analytics events.
- Notification delivery and operational records.
- Authentication/session records.
- Feedback and moderation records.
- Other personal, operational or security-sensitive data discovered during reconnaissance.

Do not assume every category can be handled by one generic deletion query.

For each proposed policy, state:

- The target data.
- The rationale.
- The retention clock.
- The proposed duration or the explicit decision needed to establish it.
- The permitted disposition.
- Dependencies and foreign-key consequences.
- Whether the data may be legally or operationally exempt from ordinary disposal.
- How successful application will be verified.

Distinguish a policy's existence from its actual execution. A table of retention policies alone does not constitute a working retention system.

